<!--
	FEMALE MERCENARIES - the appearance half. See docs/female-mercenaries.md.

	Ten vanilla female heads and seven vanilla female hairstyles, mounted in the game's MALE
	component tree so a male-bodied NPC can wear them. No new art: these are the shipped
	f_head_* / f_hair_* assets, referenced where they already live.

	WHY THIS WORKS. A head skin carries its own rig. Reading the CompiledBones chunk (type
	0x2000: a 32-byte header, then 584-byte records) out of m_head_081.skin and
	f_head_009.skin gives 360 and 364 bones, of which 139 and 142 are not per-head SSDR
	deform joints - and nearly all of those match. The facial joints that differ are named
	after the head and travel INSIDE the skin (m081_jaw, m081_tongue_01..05 against f009_jaw,
	f009_tongue_01..05), as do the ~230 *_SSDR_* joints. What a female head asks of the body
	skeleton that a male body cannot give is exactly five names: LeftBreast, LeftBreastEnd,
	RightBreast, RightBreastEnd, and a root called Human_Female rather than Human_Male. A head
	is not weighted to breast joints, so the engine skips them. Verified in game 2026-09-06:
	the head binds and renders.

	The merge shape - the chain from <Component Name="Male"> down repeated verbatim,
	intermediate <Elements> included, only the leaf new - is Rose of Bohemia's
	(references/rose_of_bohemia_main). The intermediate Elements are not decoration:
	<Head Name="Head"> carries the uberlod head skin and <Hair Name="Hair"> the uberlod hair
	skin, and a merge that omits them is a merge that may drop them.

	FilePath on a leaf REPLACES the parent's rather than appending - vanilla m_head_081
	carries FilePath="humans/male/head/m_head_081" under a parent already at "humans/male/",
	which only reads sanely as a replacement. That is what lets a component in the Male tree
	reach into humans/female/.

	NAMES. Deliberately NOT m_head_0* / m_hair_0*: vanilla's own rules
	(Libs/Storm/appearance/common/heads.xml, hair.xml) hand ordinary male NPCs
	setHead name="m_head_0*" and setHair name="m_hair_0*" by wildcard, so anything named into
	that space would start turning up on random townsmen. "merc_" matches no vanilla wildcard.

	HAIR PHYSICS is deliberately dropped. f_hair_001/003/006 carry JointElements in vanilla
	(braids, a ponytail); the entries below omit them, so those styles render stiff rather
	than swinging. That is a knowingly incomplete first pass - a dangling joint reference is a
	worse failure than an unsimulated braid, and Rose shows the joints can be added later
	(they travel with the hair skin, not the body skeleton). Four styles - 002, 004, 005, 007 -
	have no JointElements in vanilla either, so those are complete as written. The ten
	mercenaries below are spread across all seven on purpose: one `merc_hire_female 10` says
	which styles render and which do not.
-->
